 to be  active in response to this pain or a  sensory behavior you can see here very  nicely and then they recreated a map of  the cell populations that are active in  the spinal cord  now again also following up with single  nuclei profile this is another really  nice study that came out of the Broad  Institute at mga MIT where they did  single nuclei profiling of the human  Alzheimer's disease brain they created  essentially a map of the cellular map of  the Alzheimer's disease brain and so  RNAi scope was used to confirm down  regulation of some differentially  expressed genes in particular nt ng one  they found to be down regulated in the  excitatory neurons of the ad  pathological brain and those excitatory  neurons were marked by SLE C 1787  and then this last example here I wanted  to highlight was again another nice  study coming out of the Karolinska where  they created an atlas of the vagal  sensory neuron and you can see here very  very nicely I'm just showing the nodos  neurons they also did this for jugular  neurons but they basically identified  all of these various clusters of cells  and they're all shown here in the kidney  plot and then in the middle here for  every row that's a different gene and  every column is a different cluster that  they identified and so you can just see  the real the breadth of genes that are  expressed in all the different clusters  they then went in and used RNA scope to  confirm these gene signatures in each of  those clusters so again every row is a  different gene and every column is a  different cell type that they identified  and they really got very nice single  cell single molecule confirmation of  their gene expression but I think in  assays that could be particularly  helpful in this case we have so many  genes and so many cell types to analyze  is really the new RNA scope high plex  access assay which we just released this  now allows researchers to detect up to  12 RNA targets in the tissue context and  it has 